---
id: org.dxos.plugin.agent
name: AgentPlugin
version: 0.1.0
---

Manages autonomous agents in a space: assistant `Agent` objects that converse with
people in channels (Discord, freeq, local feeds) and Composer chats, interview them, and keep what they learn: facts
in the agent's brain, about the `Person` or `Organization` they name, and `Goal`s as ECHO objects.

This document records what is built. Design intent lives in [`docs/DESIGN.md`](./docs/DESIGN.md)
(architecture, EDGE gateway, identity) and [`docs/FACTS.md`](./docs/FACTS.md) (facts, profiles and
the interview skill); items there that are not built are listed under "Not built" below rather than
specified.

The plugin depends on `org.dxos.plugin.assistant` (which registers `Agent`, `Chat` and `HasSubject`
and renders the Agent article) and `org.dxos.plugin.thread`, whose channel operations carry every
message the agent sends outside Composer. It knows no channel backend: Discord lives in
plugin-discord as a `ChannelBackendProvider`. It ships a workerd variant with no React surfaces, so
EDGE's operation service can host the agent and knowledge operations.

## Extensions

The following extension dialects are used in this document. `op@1.1`, `test@1.0` and `step@1.0`
are defined in [`packages/reflect/deus/lang/qa.mdl`](../../reflect/deus/lang/qa.mdl); `node`,
`companion` and `surface` in [`packages/reflect/deus/lang/app.mdl`](../../reflect/deus/lang/app.mdl); `skill@1.0` in
[`packages/plugins/plugin-crm/PLUGIN.mdl`](../plugin-crm/PLUGIN.mdl); `decision@1.0` in
[`packages/reflect/deus/lang/changelog.mdl`](../../reflect/deus/lang/changelog.mdl); the rest in the Appendix.

| Term        | URI                            |
|-------------|--------------------------------|
| `type`      | `org.dxos.mdl.type@1.0`        |
| `feat`      | `org.dxos.mdl.feat@1.0`        |
| `scenario`  | `org.dxos.mdl.scenario@1.0`    |
| `component` | `org.dxos.mdl.component@1.0`   |
| `op`        | `org.dxos.mdl.op@1.1`          |
| `skill`     | `org.dxos.mdl.skill@1.0`       |
| `node`      | `org.dxos.mdl.node@1.0`        |
| `companion` | `org.dxos.mdl.companion@1.0`   |
| `surface`   | `org.dxos.mdl.surface@1.0`     |
| `test`      | `org.dxos.mdl.test@1.0`        |
| `step`      | `org.dxos.mdl.step@1.0`        |
| `decision`  | `org.dxos.mdl.decision@1.0`    |

## External types

Used, not defined, here: `Agent` and `Chat` (`@dxos/assistant`), `Instructions` and `Skill`
(`@dxos/compute`), `Feed` (`@dxos/echo`), `AccessToken` (`@dxos/link`), `Person`, `Organization` and
the `HasSubject` relation (`@dxos/types`), `ProfileOf` (`@dxos/plugin-crm`, registered here too so
profiles can be written without plugin-crm enabled) and `Markdown.Document` (`@dxos/plugin-markdown`).

## Types

```mdl
type AgentChannels
  desc: |
    The channels an agent converses in, whatever their backend. Typename
    `org.dxos.type.agent.channels@0.1.0`. Parented to its agent (`Obj.Parent`), so it is deleted with
    the agent; `loadForAgent` returns the first child list and `loadChannels` loads its channels.
    The backend's own settings (a Discord bot token) live on each channel's config, not here.
  fields:
    agent: Ref<Agent>               # hidden from forms; set once by make()
    channels: Ref<Channel>[]        # "Channels"
```

```mdl
type AgentChannels.Properties
  desc: The user-editable subset (channels); the Activity companion's channel picker.
  fields:
    channels: Ref<Channel>[]
```

```mdl
type Relay
  desc: |
    The delivery record of a relay. Typename `org.dxos.type.agent.relay@0.2.0` (0.1.0 held a Discord
    `replyChannelId`; relays at 0.1.0 are not migrated and no longer list). Parented to the agent.
  fields:
    task: Ref<Task>
    recipient: Ref<Obj>
    requester?: Ref<Person>
    replyChannel?: Ref<Channel>     # where the requester asked, for the report
    replyThread?: string            # the thread inside replyChannel (a Discord thread or DM)
    message: string
    dueAt: DateTime
    status: pending | delivered | reported | failed | expired
    deliveredAt?: DateTime
    reportedAt?: DateTime
    outcome?: string
```

```mdl
type RecordedFact
  desc: |
    Not a stored ECHO type: one structured fact an agent records with op:recordFacts, stored in its brain as an
    `RDF.Fact` in a named graph of its own, stated by the agent (`attribution.agent`), so a correction can
    supersede it alone. The brain holds only facts: those read from sources and those the agent recorded.
  fields:
    subject: Ref<Obj>               # stored as the entity's object URI, with its display name
    predicate: string               # a seed predicate (RDF.Ontology.DEFAULT, less dx:claim and dx:hasNote) or a new one
    predicateDescription?: string   # what a new predicate relates
    object?: Ref<Obj>               # exactly one of object and value
    value?: string                  # a literal object
    quote?: string
    origin?: stated | inferred      # default stated; factuality CT+ when stated, PR+ when inferred
    confidence?: number             # 0..1
    supersedes?: string[]           # graph IRIs of the facts it corrects
```

```mdl
type Goal
  desc: |
    A goal held by one or more people or organizations. Typename
    `org.dxos.type.agent.goal@0.1.0`. New goals are `proposed`; only the owner's confirmation
    moves them on. `owners` cannot be typed as Person | Organization in the schema, so the operations
    enforce it. Label: `title`.
  fields:
    title: string
    description?: string
    horizon: now | quarter | year | long-term
    status: proposed | confirmed | active | achieved | dropped
    owners: Ref<Obj>[]              # Person | Organization, checked by suggestGoal
    parent?: Ref<Obj>               # the goal this one serves
```

```mdl
type Profile
  desc: |
    Not a stored type — a `Markdown.Document` parented to the subject and linked to it by `ProfileOf`,
    rendered deterministically by `Profile.render` from the subject's live goals (sections Goals,
    Proposed goals, Achieved goals; dropped omitted) and the current facts about it, one section per
    predicate (its ontology label), each line the object, or the whole fact when the subject is its
    object; inferred ones are marked _(inferred)_.
    Regenerated, never edited as the source of truth.
```

## Operations

Agent operations (`AgentOperationHandlerSet`, every host including workerd).

```mdl
op createAgent
  key: org.dxos.operation.agent.create
  desc: Creates an autonomous agent with its instructions, first chat and context bindings.
  input:
    name: string
    instructions?: string           # markdown; default ''
  output: { agent: Ref<Agent> }
  effects: [echo:write]
  requires: [Database.Service]
  note: |
    `Agent.makeInitialized` with the Interview skill in the agent's Instructions skill set and the
    Agent conversation skill bound to the first chat. Settings › Agents › Add agent invokes it with the name typed
    beside it ('' shows the placeholder "New agent") and, while the plugin's "Create identities for agents" setting is
    on, follows it with op:ensureIdentity and `mint: true` (F-7.4).
```

```mdl
op ensureIdentity
  key: org.dxos.operation.agent.ensureIdentity
  desc: Gives the agent its own identity and default space on EDGE and puts the caller in that space.
  input:
    agent: Ref<Agent>
    mint?: boolean                  # provisions an agent without a DID; default false
  output: { provisioned: boolean, identityDid?: string }
  effects: [echo:write, net:edge, halo:write]
  requires: [Database.Service, Capability.Service]
  note: |
    `POST /compute/brain { agent, homeSpaceId, label }`: EDGE mints the brain's self-owned HALO identity and its
    default space (owned by that identity, named after the agent, tagged `org.dxos.space.agent`), and admits the
    caller to that space as ADMIN. The members who may write to the default space become the agent's admins (a
    reader does not); the caller, its creator, is an ADMIN, while the agent's identity owns the space, and the agent
    issues the creator its first `IdentityAdmin` credential. Members join through the space's Members panel like any
    space's members, and their clients ask EDGE to make them admins (F-7.9); admins are only added. The caller must
    be able to write to `homeSpaceId`.
    The agent takes the DID as soon as EDGE answers, since the DID names its brain and must exist before its first
    turn, and records its creator's DID and its own space (`Obj.Meta` keys `org.dxos.agent/creator` and
    `org.dxos.agent/space`). Then, in the background and retried for about two minutes, the caller's `SpaceMember`
    credential is written to their HALO (which is what makes the client join the space) and the agent's identity is
    admitted to `homeSpaceId`; if every retry fails this is only logged, and the agent keeps its DID. Invoked again
    for an agent with a DID, as opening the agent does, it finishes for the agent's creator whichever of those two
    steps did not happen (`missingJoin`): it asks EDGE again, which answers its creator with the same identity and
    membership, and runs only the missing steps. When nothing is missing, or the caller is not the creator, it asks
    EDGE nothing and changes nothing. An agent without a DID is provisioned only with `mint`, for an agent created a
    moment before: the DID names the agent's brain, so provisioning an agent that has talked would lose what it
    learned. For the same reason the operation stays out of the registry (`skipRegistry`), where models and MCP
    clients find operations. Without an EDGE client (a headless host) nothing changes. A refusal from EDGE fails the
    operation and changes nothing.
```

```mdl
op ensureChannelChat
  key: org.dxos.operation.agent.ensureChannelChat
  desc: Returns the chat mirroring a channel conversation (a channel, or a thread or DM in it), creating it on first contact.
  input:
    agent: Ref<Agent>
    channel: Ref<Channel>
    thread?: string                 # backend-scoped thread id
    title?: string                  # names a new chat
  output: { chat: Ref<Chat>, feed: Ref<Feed> }
  effects: [echo:read, echo:write]
  requires: [Database.Service]
  note: |
    Idempotent on (agent, channel, thread): the chat carries the `Obj.Meta` key
    `{ source: 'org.dxos.agent/channel', id: '<channelId>' | '<channelId>/<thread>' }`
    (`AgentChannels.chatKey`); a chat with the key parented to another agent is not a match. A new chat
    is parented to the agent, shares its Instructions, and is bound to the skills and objects of the
    agent's current chat (an agent with no chat contributes itself), plus the new chat. Called by
    sendMessage and, from step 4, by the EDGE gateway.
```

```mdl
op sendMessage
  key: org.dxos.operation.agent.sendMessage
  desc: Delivers a message to a person through the agent's conversation with them.
  input:
    agent: Ref<Agent>
    recipient: Ref<Obj>
    text: string
    relay?: Ref<Relay>
  output: { delivered: boolean, via?: chat | channel, chat?: Ref<Chat>, reason?: string }
  requires: [Database.Service]
  note: |
    In order: a report to a relay's requester goes to `relay.replyChannel`/`replyThread`; else the
    recipient's participant chat (appended, or posted through its channel when it mirrors one); else
    the first of the agent's channels whose backend opens a direct conversation (`openDirect`), which
    then becomes the person's chat. Posts go through plugin-thread's `sendToChannel` and are recorded
    in the conversation's chat with the backend's receipt properties. A backend refusal is returned
    as `{ delivered: false, reason }`.
```

```mdl
op listAgents
  key: org.dxos.operation.agent.list
  desc: Lists every Agent in the space with the number of chats parented to it.
  input: {}
  output: { agents: { agent: Ref<Agent>, name?: string, chats: number }[] }
  effects: [echo:read]
  requires: [Database.Service]
  note: Lists all `Agent` objects, not only ones this plugin created.
```

```mdl
op inviteAgent
  key: org.dxos.operation.agent.invite
  desc: Invites an agent from another space into this one; it keeps one brain across the spaces it is in.
  input:
    agent: Ref<Agent>               # in any space it is in
  output: { agent: Ref<Agent>, created: boolean }
  effects: [echo:read, echo:write, net:edge]
  requires: [Database.Service, Hypergraph.Service, Capability.Service]
  note: |
    Runs in the target space after the members panel has admitted the agent; only AgentAdmission invokes it
    (F-8.1), so it is not in the registry. It admits no one: the agent's membership is the regular admission's.
    Outside its home it registers the space with EDGE (`POST /compute/brain/:agent/spaces`), then returns the
    presence keyed `AgentPresence.homeKey(<home agent URI>)`, created on first invitation with the home's name,
    DID and instructions (`makeAgent`, so its modes and first chat match op:createAgent); in the home it returns
    the home agent. It writes no conversation. Customized skills stay home: a presence binds the compiled ones.
    The DID is the one EDGE confirms (`GET /compute/brain/:agent`), never a space's claim, since a client chooses
    its own space's tags and owner.
```

Knowledge operations (the Interview skill's tools).

```mdl
op resolveEntity
  key: org.dxos.operation.agent.resolveEntity
  desc: Finds the Person or Organization matching the handles or name, creating it if absent.
  input:
    name?: string
    handles?: { label?: string, value: string }[]   # e.g. { label: 'discord', value: '<user id>' }
    kind?: person | organization                    # default person
  output: { entity: Ref<Obj>, created: boolean }
  errors:
    AgentOperationError: neither a name nor a handle was given
  effects: [echo:read, echo:write]
  requires: [Database.Service]
  note: |
    Person: a handle match wins (value equal, and label equal when the handle has one); otherwise a
    case-insensitive match on fullName or preferredName. Handles the match lacks are appended to its
    `identities`. Organization: case-insensitive name match only; handles are ignored.
```

```mdl
op recordFacts
  key: org.dxos.operation.agent.recordFacts
  desc: Records structured facts in the agent's brain, each stated by the agent.
  input:
    agent?: Ref<Agent>              # default: the space's only agent
    facts: RecordedFact[]           # at least one
    source?: Ref<Obj>               # the message or chat they came from
  output: { facts: string[] }       # the graph IRIs, in order
  errors:
    AgentOperationError: no facts, a fact with neither or both of object and value, a prose predicate (claim, has note), confidence outside 0..1, or no agent given in a space with several
  effects: [echo:read, brain:write]
  requires: [Database.Service, BrainService]
  note: |
    Pushed as the agent's own words (`quiet: [agent]`), so it wakes no watch. The predicate list is the tool's
    `predicate` description; a predicate outside it is minted in the agent's brain namespace, as extraction mints it.
```

```mdl
op retrieveFacts
  key: org.dxos.operation.agent.retrieveFacts
  desc: Returns the current facts about a subject (newest first) and the live goals it owns.
  input:
    subject?: Ref<Obj>              # all facts and goals if omitted
    query?: string                  # every word must start a word of the fact or its quote
    limit?: number
  output: |
    { queryMatched?: false,
      facts: { id, fact, quote?, speaker?, source, sourceName?, saidAt }[],
      goals: { goal: Ref<Goal>, title, horizon, status }[] }
  effects: [echo:read, brain:read]
  requires: [Database.Service, BrainService]
  note: |
    Superseded, retracted and expired facts are left out, as are the free-text memory graphs a brain may still hold.
    A fact's id is its graph IRI, which facts read from the same words share. Live goals are every status but
    `dropped`. Facts come from the agent's brain, the one `AgentPresence.brainId` names: an agent invited from
    another space reads its home's brain, so the facts it learned in every space are one tool call away (F-6.1).
```

```mdl
op suggestGoal
  key: org.dxos.operation.agent.suggestGoal
  desc: Records a goal the owners described; it stays proposed until confirmed.
  input:
    title: string
    description?: string
    horizon: now | quarter | year | long-term
    owners: Ref<Obj>[]              # at least one; each a Person or Organization
    parent?: Ref<Goal>
  output: { goal: Ref<Goal> }
  errors:
    AgentOperationError: no owners, or an owner that is not a Person or Organization
  effects: [echo:write]
  requires: [Database.Service]
```

```mdl
op setGoalStatus
  key: org.dxos.operation.agent.setGoalStatus
  desc: Sets the status of a goal its owner has answered for.
  input:
    goal: Ref<Goal>
    status?: confirmed | active | achieved | dropped   # default confirmed
  output: { goal: Ref<Goal> }
  effects: [echo:write]
  requires: [Database.Service]
  note: No transition rules are enforced; any listed status may be set from any other.
```

```mdl
op updateProfile
  key: org.dxos.operation.agent.updateProfile
  desc: Rewrites the subject's profile document from its live goals and the current facts about it.
  input:
    subject: Ref<Obj>               # Person or Organization
  output: { document: Ref<Obj> }
  effects: [echo:read, echo:write, brain:read]
  requires: [Database.Service, BrainService]
  note: |
    Updates the existing `ProfileOf` document's text in place (and its `lastResearchedAt`);
    otherwise creates a `Markdown.Document` parented to the subject plus a `ProfileOf` relation.
```

## Skills

```mdl
skill ConversationSkill
  key: org.dxos.skill.agentConversation
  desc: Bound to every autonomous agent's first chat; the conversational role.
  contributes:
    - op:createAgent
    - op:ensureChannelChat
    - op:listAgents
  instructions: >
    Authored in `src/skills/ConversationSkill.ts`: reply in the conversation (which may mirror a
    channel conversation), address the latest message, be concise.
```

```mdl
skill InterviewSkill
  key: org.dxos.skill.interview
  desc: Interviews a person to learn their role, goals, team and preferences, and records it.
  contributes:
    - op:resolveEntity
    - op:recordFacts
    - op:retrieveFacts
    - op:suggestGoal
    - op:setGoalStatus
    - op:updateProfile
  instructions: >
    Authored in `src/skills/InterviewSkill.ts`. Start: state the purpose, resolve the person (name and
    handles), recall what is known and do not re-ask it, and — only if a checklist tool is available —
    track four topics (role, goals, team, preferences). During: one open question at a time, reflect
    back, probe each goal (why, by when, obstacles, who), record what it learns as facts (subject, listed
    predicate, entity or value) with stated or inferred origin, supersede contradictions, propose each goal. End: read the goals back, set each
    answer's status (dropped for rejections), update the person's and any team's profile, summarise.
```

```mdl
skill SupportSkill
  key: org.dxos.skill.agentSupport
  desc: Community support in a public channel; bound by the built-in Support mode.
  contributes:
    - op:resolveEntity
    - op:retrieveFacts
    - op:recordFacts
    - op:sendMessage
  instructions: >
    Authored in `src/skills/SupportSkill.ts`; also binds the relay operations (create, update). Resolve the
    asker and recall their setup and earlier answers first. A question gets the shortest correct answer, never
    an invented command, API or link. A bug report gets the missing details asked for once, a summary, and a
    "filed" fact. A feature request gets a "proposes" fact and no promise. What it cannot answer it offers to
    the team member whose expertise fits — the team and each member's areas are facts it recalls ("support
    team"), not part of the skill — and relays only once the person agrees (security, data loss, billing and
    account access go at once), with the thread as the reply channel; with no fitting member, or an
    undelivered message, it says so rather than claim a handoff. Never asks for secrets, tells anyone who
    posts one to rotate it, shares of the team only a first name and an area.
```

Both are contributed as `SkillDefinition`s; createAgent puts the Interview skill in every new agent's
Instructions. The Support skill is bound by the built-in Support mode.

## App graph

```mdl
node AgentsSection
  desc: |
    Settings › Agents, under the space's settings beside Members and Connections: the space's agents, each opening
    its article, and Add agent, which creates one with the name typed beside it ('' shows the placeholder "New agent")
    through `containers/AgentsSettings/create-agent.ts` and opens it. `Agent` is not a user type, so agents are not listed with the
    space's content and "Add to space" does not offer them.
  source: node:SpaceRoot
  subject: type:Agent
  actions: [op:createAgent]
  note: |
    Add agent gives the new agent its identity (op:ensureIdentity) only while the plugin's "Create identities for
    agents" setting is on (F-7.4). The name is asked for before the agent exists because EDGE names the agent's
    identity and its own space when it mints them.
```

```mdl
node AccountAgents
  desc: |
    The account's Agents panel (`dxos:account/agents`, URL key `agents`), one of the account's panels beside
    plugin-client's. Its `position` is `Position.last`, so the panel is listed after them.
  source: dxos:account
  properties:
    label: Agents
    icon: ph--robot--regular
  article: surface:AccountAgents
```

## Components

```mdl
component AgentAdmission
  desc: |
    Capability (capabilities/agent-admission.ts), no UI: it follows `SpaceOperation.AddMembers`. A space's Members
    panel admits an agent's identity as it admits any contact's, and its picker offers only the agents the member
    administers (`AppSpace.agentsNotAdministeredBy`). For each admitted agent this client's identity administers
    (`AgentSpace.administeredAgents`, by the home its own space records), other than the space's own agent, it
    invokes op:inviteAgent there, retrying a few times; an agent EDGE refuses stays a member that does not answer.
  actions:
    admitted(agent) → op:inviteAgent
```

```mdl
component ProfileGraph
  desc: |
    Two labelled regions, "Goals" (title; horizon · status) and "Facts" (the fact; predicate · origin ·
    date), or "Nothing recorded yet." when both are empty.
  props:
    goals: Goal[]
    facts: { id, text, predicate, origin?, saidAt }[]
```

```mdl
component AgentActivity
  desc: |
    Pure activity panel (components/AgentActivity), a composite. Root: Panel over a scrolling body.
    Channels: an auto-saving form over AgentChannels.Properties (a picker over the space's Channels)
    followed by its children (each channel's backend settings). Skills and Skill: the bound skills
    with customize/reset. Conversations: a "Conversations" list, or "No channel conversations yet."
    when empty. Conversation: one row (chat icon, title or "Untitled conversation", compact
    last-activity Timestamp) that emits onSelect.
  props:
    values?: Partial<AgentChannels.Properties>
    conversations: { id: string, title?: string, lastActivity?: string }[]
  emits:
    onSave(values: AgentChannels.Properties)
    onSelect(id: string)
```

```mdl
component AgentActivityContainer
  desc: |
    Container (containers/AgentActivity) wiring AgentActivity to the agent. Finds the agent's
    AgentChannels and its Chats with child-of queries (`Filter.and(Filter.type(T), Filter.childOf(agent))`,
    never `.children()` — EDGE cannot run traversals). With no list, saving adds one parented to the
    agent (`db.add`, no operation); with one, edits auto-save into it. Under the picker, each channel
    renders the generic `ObjectProperties` surface for the Channel, so its backend plugin shows its
    settings (plugin-discord: the bot form, status and Start/Stop). Lists the chats carrying an
    `org.dxos.agent/channel` key, newest first by id; each row subscribes to its own chat name and
    feed so last activity is the newest message's `created`. Selecting a row opens the chat.
  props:
    agent: Agent
  actions:
    select(chat) → org.dxos.operation.appToolkit.open
```

```mdl
component AgentList
  desc: |
    The agents the viewer administers (components/AgentList), in the contacts book's rows: per agent, its avatar, its
    name ("Unnamed agent" when it has none), a chip per space the agent is a member of that the viewer is too, each
    opening that space and coloured by the hash of the space's id, and its DID with a copy button. A filter keeps the
    agents whose name, identity key or DID contains it. With none, a line saying how to get one. It copies the rows of
    `ContactList` in `@dxos/shell` until the two are factored into one.
  props:
    agents: Contact[]
    spaces: { id: string, key: PublicKey, name?: string }[]
    filter?: string
  emits:
    onSelectSpace(space)
```

```mdl
component AgentsPanel
  desc: |
    Container (containers/AgentsPanel) wiring AgentList to the client, as the contacts book's container does: a search
    field once there is more than one agent, then the contacts the identity administers as agents
    (`AgentSpace.administeredAgents` over the client's spaces), each with the spaces it shares with the viewer, none
    where the agent was removed. Re-read whenever the spaces, the identity, the contacts or a space's members change.
  actions:
    open(spaceId) → org.dxos.operation.appToolkit.switchWorkspace
```

```mdl
component ProfileProperties
  desc: |
    ProfileGraph over the subject's live goals (owner refers to the subject; not dropped) and the current
    facts whose subject or object is the subject, newest first. Facts are read from the
    brains of the space's agents every 3s (EDGE's for an agent with a chat there); every Goal atom is
    subscribed, so status changes re-render without a membership change.
  props:
    subject: Person | Organization
```

## Surfaces

```mdl
companion AgentActivity
  of: type:Agent
  variant: activity
  component: component:AgentActivityContainer
  properties:
    label: Activity
    icon: ph--pulse--regular
```

```mdl
surface AgentActivity
  role: article
  subject: type:Agent               # the companion's `companionTo`; matched with the `activity` literal
  variant: activity
  component: component:AgentActivityContainer
```

```mdl
surface PersonProfileGraph
  role: objectProperties
  subject: type:Person
  component: component:ProfileProperties
  position: last
```

```mdl
surface OrganizationProfileGraph
  role: objectProperties
  subject: type:Organization
  component: component:ProfileProperties
  position: last
```

```mdl
surface AccountAgents
  desc: Matched on the panel's path literal, `dxos:account/agents`, not a type.
  role: article
  component: component:AgentsPanel
```

## Features

```mdl
feat F-1: Agents

  req F-1.1: A space's Settings › Agents lists every Agent in the space; Add agent creates an autonomous agent.
  req F-1.2:
    when: op:createAgent runs
    then: an Agent exists with Instructions holding the Interview skill and a first chat bound to the Agent conversation skill
  req F-1.3: op:listAgents reports each Agent with the number of chats parented to it.
  req F-1.4: A space's agents are added, opened and deleted in Settings › Agents; "Add to space" does not offer them.
  req F-1.5: A member has one private chat with an agent in a space. Opening the agent again while the first open is still making that chat waits for it, and when two opens each made one (two devices at once), the article opens the one with the latest message.
```

```mdl
feat F-2: Channel conversations

  req F-2.1:
    when: op:ensureChannelChat is called twice for the same agent, channel and thread
    then: the same chat and feed are returned
  req F-2.2:
    when: a new conversation arrives
    then: a new chat is parented to the agent, named after the conversation, sharing the agent's Instructions, keyed by channel and thread
  req F-2.3: The same conversation bridged to two agents yields two chats.
  req F-2.4: A channel and a thread inside it are separate conversations.
```

```mdl
feat F-3: Agent channels and delivery

  req F-3.1: An Agent's Activity companion shows a channel picker; the first save creates an AgentChannels parented to the agent.
  req F-3.2: The list is deleted with its agent and found from it by AgentChannels.loadForAgent.
  req F-3.3: Under the picker, each channel shows its backend's ObjectProperties surface (plugin-discord: bot form, status, Start/Stop).
  req F-3.4:
    when: op:sendMessage reaches a person with no chat
    then: the first of the agent's channels whose backend opens a direct conversation posts it, and that conversation becomes the person's chat
  req F-3.5:
    when: a relay's requester is reported to
    then: the report goes to the relay's replyChannel and replyThread first
  req F-3.6:
    when: the backend refuses, or no channel can reach the person
    then: op:sendMessage returns delivered false with a reason the agent can relay, and records nothing
  req F-3.7: The Activity companion lists the agent's channel chats newest first, with title and last activity; selecting one opens the chat.
```

```mdl
feat F-4: Knowledge graph

  req F-4.1: op:resolveEntity matches a Person by handle first, then case-insensitively by name, and creates one only when nothing matches.
  req F-4.2: op:recordFacts writes each fact in a named graph of its own, stated by the agent, and rejects an empty list, a fact without exactly one of object and value, and a prose predicate.
  req F-4.3:
    when: a fact is recorded with supersedes
    then: the facts it names are no longer recalled
  req F-4.4: op:retrieveFacts returns current facts only, filtered by subject and query, and the subject's live goals.
  req F-4.5: op:suggestGoal creates a proposed goal and rejects owners that are not people or organizations.
  req F-4.6: op:setGoalStatus confirms a goal by default.
  req F-4.7: op:updateProfile keeps one profile document per subject and rewrites it in place.
```

```mdl
feat F-5: Interview and profile panel

  req F-5.1: An agent with the Interview skill resolves the interviewee, records facts and proposes goals as the conversation runs, and confirms goals at the end.
  req F-5.2: A Person's or Organization's properties list its live goals and the current facts about it.
  req F-5.3:
    when: a goal's status changes or a fact is recorded
    then: the properties panel updates without reopening
```

```mdl
feat F-6: One brain per agent

  req F-6.1: An agent's brain is named by its identity's DID (`AgentPresence.brainId`), which every presence of it carries, so every space it is in shares one brain: its facts, its triggers and its wakes. An agent without an identity keeps a brain named by its home agent's id. EDGE runs the plugin-agent its catalog pins, so its turns do this once edge takes this version.
  req F-6.2: A watch is delivered in the space it was set from, where its goal and recipient live. `TriggerOperation.WatchFacts` records the agent there as the watch's origin, and an agent delivers only the watches set in its own space, so a turn elsewhere leaves them queued. EDGE's brain delivers those from its alarm, a minute after the facts that woke them or when their time comes, by running `TriggerOperation.RunDue` as the agent in each such space it still serves. A watch stored before watches recorded their origin is delivered at the agent's home. The in-app brain has no alarm, so there such a watch waits until a turn in its own space wakes a watch.
```

```mdl
feat F-7: An agent's own space

  req F-7.1: An agent with an identity has a default space its identity owns, minted with it by op:ensureIdentity and tagged `AppSpace.AGENT_SPACE_TAG`; the identity is its owner, named after the agent, and every other member who may write to it (an admin or an editor, not a reader) becomes one of the agent's admins. EDGE checks the admin role against the `IdentityAdmin` credentials the agent's identity issues, one per admin and kept in its HALO (`dxos.halo.credentials.IdentityAdmin { identity_key, admin_key }`), not against the space, and the admins' clients ask EDGE for one for each member who may write (F-7.9). Admins are only added: a member made a reader or removed stays an admin, since space membership has no revocation yet.
  req F-7.2: An agent with an identity is a member of the shared spaces it is in. op:ensureIdentity admits its identity to the space the agent is created in, as an editor named after the agent, and the members panel admits it to the spaces it is added to, as any contact (F-8.1). Only a space's admins can admit members, and a locked space (a personal or private one, `MembershipPolicy.LOCKED`) admits no one after its genesis, an agent no more than a person: an agent created there, or by anyone but an admin, works without the membership, and no one can add one to such a space.
  req F-7.3: With the setting on (F-7.4), creating an agent waits for op:ensureIdentity's answer from EDGE, about a second, so the agent opens with its DID and its brain is named by it from the first turn. EDGE names the identity and the agent's space after the agent's name at that moment, and renaming the agent later renames neither. Joining its own space and admitting it to the space it was created in follow in the background. When EDGE cannot provision it within 15 seconds, the agent opens without an identity. When those background steps fail every retry, opening the agent finishes them for its creator, and asks EDGE nothing when nothing is missing.
  req F-7.4: Agent identities are switched on by the plugin's "Create identities for agents" setting (`AgentSettings.Settings.identities`), which is off by default. Off, creating an agent asks EDGE nothing: the agent is an object in its space whose brain is named by its id, as agents always were. On, creating an agent gives it its identity (F-7.3). An agent keeps the identity it was given when the setting is turned off.
  req F-7.5: `AgentSpace.administeredBy` lists the agents an identity administers: the agent spaces among the client's own spaces, which its HALO holds memberships of, that the identity is an admin of. It asks nothing of EDGE.
  req F-7.6: `AgentSpace.admins` lists an agent's admins, the admins and editors of its space, and `AgentSpace.agentOf` is the agent as its space shows it. Anything acting on the agent's identity, such as its DID on a presence, takes it from EDGE instead (op:inviteAgent).
  req F-7.7: While the plugin's setting creates agents with identities (F-7.4), the account's Agents panel, listed after the account's other panels, lists the agents the identity administers (F-7.5) in the rows the contacts book gives people: each one's avatar, name and DID, and a chip per space it is a member of that the viewer is too, each switching to that space. It stays current as spaces and their members change.
  req F-7.8: EDGE keys an agent space's properties to the agent's URI (`AgentPresence.HOME_SOURCE`) when it mints the space. `AgentSpace.administeredAgents` reads each administered agent's name, DID and home from its space, so an admin who is not a member of the agent's home space can still add the agent to other spaces (F-8.1).
  req F-7.9: An admin's client asks EDGE to make each member who may write to an agent's space (its admins and editors) the agent's admin (`AgentAdminSync`, `POST /compute/brain/:did/admins { identityKey }`), and the agent issues each one an `IdentityAdmin` in its HALO. It never asks EDGE to take an admin off: a member made a reader or removed keeps the role. It acts only on a space EDGE names as the agent's own and only while EDGE counts the identity as the agent's admin, so a space that merely claims to be an agent's adds no one. Each add is sent once a session, and again after a failure; a client whose services reconnect starts over, since they may hold another identity. Sending an add again is harmless: EDGE appends an admin's credential when the agent's HALO lacks it, and never a second one. EDGE's 404 (an EDGE without the admins route, or a caller with no role on the agent) settles an add for the session, and another refusal (a 4xx) is sent again on the next pass. When EDGE could not be reached or failed (no answer, or a 5xx), the sync runs again 30 seconds later even if nothing changes, so an add reaches EDGE without waiting for another change; a space EDGE could not be asked about is asked again the same way.
```

```mdl
feat F-8: An agent in several spaces

  req F-8.1: While the plugin's setting creates agents with identities (F-7.4), an agent is added to a space like any contact: a space's Members panel offers, in its "Add from contacts" picker, the agents the member administers, as contacts, and leaves out the agents the member does not (`AppSpace.agentsNotAdministeredBy`); Add admits the agent's identity, and AgentAdmission then brings the agent into the space (op:inviteAgent), including one whose home space the member cannot reach.
  req F-8.2:
    when: op:inviteAgent runs for an agent already in the space (its home, or an earlier presence)
    then: that agent is returned and nothing is created
  req F-8.3: A presence carries its home's name, DID and instructions, keyed to the home agent's URI; inviting a presence keys the new one to the same home. An admin who cannot reach the home invites from the agent's own space instead (F-7.8), and the presence starts from the base instructions for its name.
  req F-8.4:
    when: a member opens an agent in a space it was invited to
    then: its article is the member's private chat with the agent in that space, a conversation of its own that the agent's other spaces do not show, and its companions show that space's goals, watches and channels; what the agent knows comes from its one brain (F-6.1), whichever space the member talks to it in
  req F-8.5: Inviting writes no conversation: nothing is quoted from any chat into the space.
  req F-8.6: Only an agent's admins can add it to another space. The picker offers only the agents the member administers, and op:inviteAgent registers the space with EDGE, which EDGE refuses anyone else, before it makes the presence; EDGE's brain serves only its home and the spaces registered so, so a presence made any other way reads and writes nothing of the brain, and EDGE runs no turn of its chats.
```

## Acceptance

Derived criteria, each executed by a vitest case or a storybook play function.

```mdl
scenario T-1: Channel chats are idempotent per conversation   # ensure-channel-chat.test.ts
  # covers [F-2.1, F-2.2, F-2.4]
  given: an agent created by op:createAgent and a channel on a test backend
  when: ensureChannelChat for thread-1 (title Hello), again for thread-1, then for thread-2 and for the channel itself
  then:
    - both thread-1 calls return the same chat and feed; thread-2 and the channel each return another
    - the thread chat is not the agent's first chat, is named Hello and is parented to the agent
    - it shares the agent's Instructions; its only meta key is AgentChannels.chatKey(channel, thread-1)
```

```mdl
scenario T-2: Conversations are separate per agent   # ensure-channel-chat.test.ts
  # covers [F-2.3, F-1.3]
  given: agents First and Second and one channel
  when: each ensures a chat for thread "shared"
  then:
    - the two chats differ
    - listAgents reports First and Second with 2 chats each
```

```mdl
scenario T-3: Channels belong to their agent   # ensure-channel-chat.test.ts
  # covers [F-3.2]
  given: an agent and a channel
  when: AgentChannels.make for the agent with the channel is added
  then: its parent is the agent, loadForAgent returns it and loadChannels returns the channel
```

```mdl
scenario T-4: An interview builds the graph   # interview.test.ts
  # covers [F-4.1, F-4.2, F-4.3, F-4.4, F-4.5, F-4.6, F-4.7]
  when: resolve "Rich Burdon" with a discord handle, then by handle, then by "rich burdon"; resolve the
    organization "Composer team"; record three facts; propose two goals and confirm one; supersede
    one fact; recall; update the profile twice
  then:
    - the first resolve creates a Person; the other two return it
    - recall returns the three current facts, not the superseded one, and both goals (confirmed, proposed)
    - recall with query "STANDUPS" returns only the matching fact; the team recalls only the fact relating it and no goals
    - both updateProfile calls return the same document, with exactly one ProfileOf
    - the profile contains the name, the confirmed goal and the new fact under its predicate, and not the superseded one
```

```mdl
scenario T-5: Invalid fact and goal input is rejected   # interview.test.ts
  # covers [F-4.2, F-4.5]
  then:
    - recordFacts with no facts, with both object and value, or with the predicate "claim" fails
    - suggestGoal owned by a Text object fails with "people or organizations"
```

```mdl
scenario T-6: Relays travel through channels   # relay.test.ts (test channel backend)
  # covers [F-3.4, F-3.5, F-3.6]
  then:
    - a person with a handle but no chat is reached by a direct thread on the agent's channel, which becomes their chat with the backend's receipt on the recorded message
    - the report to the requester goes to the relay's replyChannel and replyThread
    - a refused direct message returns the backend's reason and records no chat
    - an agent with no channels, or a person with no handle, is not delivered to, with a reason
```

```mdl
scenario T-8: Scripted interview fills the profile panel
  # covers [F-5.1, F-5.2, F-5.3]
